<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Zalpa</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 BCE – 1650 BCE (approx.) · capital Zalpa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Zalpa, a city-state on the Black Sea coast that rivalled Nesa and figures in the oldest Hittite legend, the tale of the queen of Kanesh's thirty sons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zalpuwa lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Kızılırmak</a> river. In the early 2nd millennium BCE it was one of the native Anatolian kingdoms competing for power with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kanesh">Kanesh</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> remembered it in a legend of thirty sons of the queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kanesh</a> who drifted there in baskets.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is known of its government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta</a> text records that Uhna, king of Zalpuwa, carried off the statue of the god of Nesa, and that Anitta later captured Huzziya of Zalpuwa and brought the statue home.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Zalpa resisted the early Hittite kings for years. According to the Hittite Zalpa text, it was besieged and finally destroyed in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a>, c. 1650 BCE, and its land became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1900 BCE – 1650 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:21.89%;width:4.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 BCE – 1650 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zalpa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Uhna, Huzziya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hattic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zalpuwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1775&amp;focus=zalpa" aria-label="Zalpa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 794 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zalpa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 1050h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5842 1050h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-1775&amp;focus=zalpa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

